scope: |
  `a-gate-that-starts-a-server-cannot-prove-it-is-talking-to-its-own` is `confirmed: no` — the
  mechanism is real, the instance hypothetical. It asks for identity, and for a PLANT as the proof:
  a foreign listener on the gate's port + the gate's own launcher broken must read RED.

  Ownership, not a nonce and not an allocated port, and the reason is measured: the port is written
  in the EXAMPLE (`examples/health-defaults.ssc` ends in `serve(8769)`), so both of the entry's
  suggestions edit `examples/` to fix a harness problem. Asking the OS which process holds the
  socket needs nothing from the served program. Compared as a process TREE rather than a pid,
  because `bin/ssc` is a launcher script and whether the JVM is the same pid or a child differs by
  lane — a pid comparison would be testing `exec` versus `&`.

  WIDENED to `serve-view-frontend-v2-smoke.sh` before wiring it: that is the gate where the check is
  DECISIVE rather than defence-in-depth. It asserts `http=200` plus a `frontend=` string scraped from
  its own LOG, so a foreign server returning any 200 makes it pass. The gates with body assertions
  (`route-params`, `health-defaults`) would catch a foreign server by its wrong answer; this one
  cannot. Wiring it is what turns the entry's `confirmed: no` into a demonstrated instance.
next: wire the four named gates + serve-view, plant against each, record which were verified
